latest_plan = "Based on the refined understanding of the color transformation rule, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Connected Regions:\n   a. Iterate through the grid.\n   b. When a non-black cell is found, use a flood fill algorithm (depth-first search or breadth-first search) to identify all connected cells of the same color.\n   c. Store each region as a list of cell coordinates.\n\n2. Analyze Color Layers for Each Region:\n   a. For each region, create a list to store color layers.\n   b. Start from the outer edges of the region and move inward.\n   c. Add each new color encountered to the list, maintaining the order from outermost to innermost.\n\n3. Transform Colors:\n   a. For each region's color layer list:\n      - If the list has only one color, keep it unchanged.\n      - If the list has more than one color:\n        * Move the last color (innermost) to the front of the list (new outermost).\n        * Shift all other colors one position towards the end of the list.\n\n4. Create a New Grid:\n   a. Initialize a new grid with the same dimensions as the input grid.\n   b. Set all cells to black (0) initially.\n\n5. Apply Transformed Colors:\n   a. For each region:\n      - Iterate through the cells of the region.\n      - Assign colors from the transformed color layer list, starting from the outermost and moving inward.\n      - Ensure that the overall structure and shape of each region remain unchanged.\n\n6. Preserve Black Cells:\n   a. Iterate through the original grid.\n   b. Where the original grid has black cells (0), ensure the new grid also has black cells in the same positions.\n\n7. Return the New Grid:\n   a. After processing all regions and preserving black cells, return the new grid as the result.\n\nImplementation Details:\n\n- Use a set or boolean array to keep track of visited cells during region identification to avoid reprocessing.\n- Implement the flood fill algorithm recursively or using a stack/queue for efficiency.\n- Use a deque (double-ended queue) data structure for the color layer list to efficiently move the innermost color to the front.\n- When applying transformed colors, use distance from the region's edge to determine which color to apply to each cell.\n\nThis plan should correctly implement the color transformation rule for all cases, regardless of the complexity of the regions or the number of colors involved. It maintains the structure of the original grid while applying the specific color shift pattern we've identified."
